  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Through FY2026 to date, the Department of Energy has $46B in contract obligations across 3,881 awards, with an average award size of $11.9M. Obligations fell 5.2% year over year. The FY series shows spending rising from $37.7B in FY21 to $48.5B in FY25 before leveling to $46B in FY26. Spending is heavily concentrated in one category: Facilities Support Services accounts for $23.2B, roughly half of the current-year total. The next-largest categories are Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences at $6.9B and a related R&D category at $3.4B. Remediation Services and chemical manufacturing also appear among the top categories, at $2.9B and $2.8B respectively. Large management-and-operations style awards dominate the vendor list: TRIAD NATIONAL SECURITY, LLC holds $5.1B, and NATIONAL TECHNOLOGY & ENGINEERING SOLUTIONS OF SANDIA, LLC holds $4.5B. Several national lab management contractors and prime integrators appear in the top ten, reflecting DOE’s reliance on long-term site management and R&D contracts. Note that current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date).",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Where is DOE spending concentrated?",
        "body": "Spending is concentrated in Facilities Support Services at $23.2B, which is the single largest category. R&D categories together account for significant dollars, led by Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences at $6.9B and a related R&D category at $3.4B. Remediation Services and Other Basic Inorganic Chemical Manufacturing add $2.9B and $2.8B respectively, so cleanup and chemical work are also meaningful shares."
      },
      {
        "heading": "How has DOE contract spending changed over recent years?",
        "body": "Obligations increased from $37.7B in FY21 to $48.5B in FY25 and then moved to $46B in FY26. For the current year-to-date period, obligations fell 5.2% year over year. The FY series shows a peak in FY25 followed by a partial-year leveling in FY26."
      }
    ]
  },
